"use strict";(self.webpackChunksimrantcg=self.webpackChunksimrantcg||[]).push([[12],{12:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('[{"type":"title","content":"Lost March"},{"type":"image","src":"/decks/sum-lot/lostmarch/cover.jpg"},{"type":"imageCaption","content":"nobody wants to see Marshall no more"},{"type":"heading","content":"Concept"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Night March reborn in all but name, Lost March attempts the same gimmick in a slower format. The Pokemon in Lost March place themselves in the Lost Zone, so you can focus on just setting up as many attackers as possible as your Skiploom and Trumbeak fling themselves into the Lost Zone, powering up your Jumpluff for a big \u201cLost March\u201d."},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Playing a lot of burnable Item cards, this version of Lost March looks to abuse Oranguru\'s \u201cInstruct\u201d as mid-turn draw and can use Marshadow\'s \u201cLet Loose\u201d to disrupt your opponent and draw more burnable pieces, as you pummel your opponent with back to back 200+ Damage attacks."},{"type":"heading","content":"The Deck"},{"type":"image","src":"/decks/sum-lot/lostmarch/decklist.jpg"},{"type":"link","href":"https://limitlesstcg.com/decks/list/1925","content":"Decklist on LimitlessTCG"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"List credit to Connor Finton at Mexico SPE 2018, -1 Super Boost Energy {*} +1 Basic Grass Energy."},{"type":"heading","content":"Lost March Pokemon"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"The \u201cLost March\u201d differs from its predecessor by simply counting the number of Pokemon in the Lost Zone, rather than needing said Pokemon to have any specific attributes. Thusly the deck does not need to cap at 4 copies of each Lost Marcher, and instead balances attackers and Pokemon that send themselves to the Lost Zone."},{"type":"image","src":"/decks/sum-lot/lostmarch/marchers.jpg"},{"type":"imageCaption","content":"20 billion damage incoming"},{"type":"subheading","content":"4-4-4 Hoppip, Skiploom, Jumpluff"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"The Jumpluff line is the most critical one in the deck, you\'ll want as many Hoppip down turn 1 as you can get. Skiploom\'s \u201cFloral Path to the Sky\u201d puts itself and the Hoppip below it into the Lost Zone, fuelling the strength of the Jumpluff that takes its place. Although Jumpluff only has 70HP you can handle spread from Tapu Koko by staggering uses of Skiploom\'s Ability, as the Jumpluff that comes into play does not retain the damage counters on the Skiploom."},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Each Skiploom adds 40 Damage to Jumpluff\'s \u201cLost March\u201d, which will add up fast if you can get 2-3 Skiploom evolved turn 2. Having free Retreat enables easier Guzma plays and the ability to pivot to Natu should you fail to find a Grass Energy."},{"type":"subheading","content":"3 Natu"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Secondary attacker Natu is notably easier to get into play, but only has 40HP and doesn\'t fuel the Lost Zone. Natu hits for relevant Psychic Weakness, but is also resisted by Zoroark GX! Natu attacks for Double Colorless Energy, so weave Natu in between Jumpluff to get the best use of your resources."},{"type":"subheading","content":"4 Trumbeak"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Trumbeak, I\'d wager, is the funniest card in the Pokemon Card Game. As a +20 Damage modifier to your \u201cLost March\u201d attacks, it serves the explicit purpose of going to the Lost Zone via it\'s Ability \u201cMountain Pass\u201d. It just also has the side effect of giving you knowledge of your opponent\'s topdeck, and can highroll Suporter cards into the discard pile. In practice, a use of \u201cMountain Pass\u201d might give you a better idea whether to then play \u201cLet Loose\u201d Marshadow or not."},{"type":"heading","content":"Support Pokemon"},{"type":"subheading","content":"2 Oranguru"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Oranguru\'s \u201cInstruct\u201d might not look mighty, but it plays incredibly clean. Refilling your hand 1-2 cards at least once a turn can help you dig for more Trumbeak and Skiploom to get to a OHKO. Don\'t rely entirely on \u201cInstruct\u201d to draw, leave that to one of the shuffle draw Supporters in the deck, but play into it when you need a huge pop off turn. Note that Marshadow\'s \u201cLet Loose\u201d can get you closer to that small hand size you need to use Oranguru."},{"type":"image","src":"/decks/sum-lot/lostmarch/lose.jpg"},{"type":"imageCaption","content":"Let Lose!"},{"type":"subheading","content":"2 Marshadow"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"No one likes a turn 1 \u201cLet Loose\u201d. And with Oranguru on board, you can happily send it every game the pieces align in your hand. Use Marshadow to disrupt and redraw when your hand gets jammed with Jumpluff you need in the Deck. Note that committing to multiple Oranguru and/or Marshadow will leave you with little Bench space to play down your actual attacking Pokemon."},{"type":"heading","content":"Trainers"},{"type":"subheading","content":"Search & Draw"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Many Lost March decklists at the time ran an Elm\'s engine, as the card searched Hoppip, Skiploom or Natu out of the deck. This version however opts for heavy draw Supporters, allowing you to find other pieces, whilst putting the work of finding Pokemon on the heap of Item search cards in the deck. With so many Pokemon to hit, the 4 Great Ball and 2 Timer Ball are almost always useful. 2 Nest Ball and 2 Net Ball help find Basics or situationally Grass Energy, and the all-rounder Ultra Ball makes an appearance as a 3 count, owing to its heavy discard cost."},{"type":"image","src":"/decks/sum-lot/lostmarch/ball.jpg"},{"type":"imageCaption","content":"This list plays a staggering 13 Ball cards!"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"For further consistency 4 Cynthia keep you drawing every turn, whilst the 3 Lillie can be used turn 1 for a burst draw to 8 or simply to refresh an emptied hand."},{"type":"subheading","content":"Gust, Lost Zone Ramp, Recovery & Damage Modification"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"The remaining Trainer Cards in the deck all fall under the above in some way."},{"type":"paragraph","content":"3 Guzma gust up easy 2 Prize KOs, which keeps you comfortably ahead in the prize exchange."},{"type":"paragraph","content":"3 Lost Blender allow you to Lost Zone extra Oranguru/Marshadow/Jumpluff/Natu in a pinch. "},{"type":"paragraph","content":"The 2 Rescue Stretcher are critical for recovering Jumpluff to get max uses of Skiploom, but can also just grab a Marshadow or Oranguru you need the moment you need it."},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Finally the 1 Choice Band is a once per game damage amp, notably useful for early turns where you don\'t have the Pokemon in the Lost Zone to OHKO a larger Pokemon GX."},{"type":"heading","content":"Energy"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"As all attackers in the deck are single Energy attackers, the deck plays only 8 Energy cards. This list plays 4 Double Colorless Energy to have maximum outs to attack with Natu, and plays 4 Grass Energy to attack with Jumpluff. The Grass Energy can be searched with Net Ball, another reason for the low count."}]')}}]);